eaming_fp32_lanes": 16, "capabilities": ["cpu", "arm64", "sme", "sme2", "neon", "simd", "single-thread"], "sme2": true, "p_core_l1d_bytes": 131072, "e_core_l1d_bytes": 65536, "cache_line_bytes": 128}, "host": {"timestamp_utc": "2026-09-25T09:29:26.006218+00:00", "battery": "Now drawing from 'Battery Power'\n -InternalBattery-0 (id=21889123)\t47%; discharging; 2:30 remaining present: true\n", "power_settings": "Battery Power:\n powermode            1\n Sleep On Power Button 1\n standby              1\n ttyskeepawake        1\n hibernatemode        3\n powernap             0\n hibernatefile        /var/vm/sleepimage\n displaysleep         10\n womp                 0\n networkoversleep     0\n sleep                3\n tcpkeepalive         1\n lessbright           1\n disksleep            10\nAC Power:\n Sleep On Power Button 1\n powermode            0\n standby              1\n ttyskeepawake        1\n hibernatemode        3\n powernap             1\n hibernatefile        /var/vm/sleepimage\n displaysleep         10\n womp                 1\n networkoversleep     0\n sleep                5\n tcpkeepalive         1\n disksleep            10\n", "thermal": "Note: No thermal warning level has been recorded\nNote: No performance warning level has been recorded\nNote: No CPU power status has been recorded\n"}, "status": "final-performance-unconfirmed", "attempts": [{"experiment_id": "n512_fast_path", "status": "proposed", "name": "libxsmm-n512-sixteen-panel32-calls", "hypothesis": "Replace the n=512 full-kernel call with sixteen supplied panel32 calls. Bands r=0,32,...,480 cover all output rows exactly once; slots [4]=B, [10]=A+r*512 and [16]=C+r*512 implement the supplied row-major mapping. Other slots remain zero. This tests 64 KiB per-call packing against additional call and SME transition costs. No fallible setup is added; the original full512 kernel remains available unchanged. General sizes, tails, n<=0, FP32 arithmetic and both complete license notices remain unchanged. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness and performance are untested. Promotion requires fresh paired controls under the fixed gates and unchanged battery power/settings; throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is not established.", "source_hash": "0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051"}, {"experiment_id": "panel_packing", "status": "abstained", "reason": "The selected wrapper change was already tested as libxsmm-n512-sixteen-panel32-calls, source hash 0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051. It uses the same sixteen row bands and parameter mapping. Correctness passed, but scores of 1078.1\u20131090.8 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 1067.0\u20131112.3 established no repeatable gain. It was not promoted and did not exceed 1800 GFLOP/s. No duplicate implementation or substitute experiment is submitted."}, {"experiment_id": "cache_blocking", "status": "abstained", "reason": "The selected experiment duplicates libxsmm-n512-sixteen-panel32-calls, source hash 0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051. That source already uses the exact sixteen-band loop and parameter mapping requested. Recorded correctness passed. Candidate scores were 1078.1\u20131090.8 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 1067.0\u20131112.3; it was not promoted. These results establish no repeatable gain or cache cause, and no repeat exceeded 1800 GFLOP/s. Stop to avoid repeating the tested experiment."}, {"experiment_id": "constant_shape_512", "status": "proposed", "name": "libxsmm-n512-constant-shape-c-sme", "hypothesis": "Route n=512 through the existing full A transpose and a guarded C SME implementation with fixed 32x32 tiles and 512 K steps. Each tile remains in ZA for its full FP32 accumulation. The guard requires 16 streaming FP32 lanes; other vector lengths retain the general loop. Allocation failure, other sizes, tails and n<=0 retain existing fallback behavior. This tests compiler-visible constant bounds. The full512 assembly, panel helper and complete license notices remain unchanged. This source is absent from the supplied measured history. Compilation, correctness and performance are untested; no gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established. Validate the full timed GEMM under the fixed gates with fresh repeated paired battery controls and unchanged settings. Results compare the complete changed path and cannot isolate constant bounds from the change in implementation.", "source_hash": "cadb05ebb2492c7eda03d5a5e2a71eca07ce0a5bcfd4e4a83053f708a3c598b7"}, {"experiment_id": "cache_conflict_layout", "status": "abstained", "reason": "Cannot implement the selected layout as specified. The supplied full512 kernel packs 64 FP32 values per K slice: a 256-byte stride and 128 KiB total. A 2,112-byte stride reserves 1,081,344 bytes across 512 slices, not 128 KiB plus 8 KiB. Also, 512 FP32 values plus 32 padding values require 2,176 bytes, not 2,112. The stated 65,664-byte footprint does not resolve these conflicts. An 8 KiB total increase would instead imply a 272-byte K stride. Choosing one of these incompatible layouts would change the selected experiment. No edits or tests were performed; no performance claim is made."}, {"experiment_id": "cache_conflict_layout", "status": "proposed", "name": "libxsmm-full512-padded-272-byte-a-slices", "hypothesis": "Implement the selected padding experiment in the full512 kernel: allocate 139264 bytes, pack 512 slices at 272-byte intervals, and update both packed-A consumer strides and rewinds. Each slice contains 256 data bytes followed by 16 unused bytes. The second row half still starts at byte 128; the final data byte is at offset 139247, within the allocation. B and C addressing, FP32 FMOPA order, one kernel call, SME transitions, general-size fallback, and both complete license notices remain unchanged. This corrected layout is absent from the supplied measured history. The hypothesis is that padding changes scratch cache mapping; cache geometry and benefit are unverified. No tools or tests were run. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness, and performance require fresh validation under the fixed gates and repeated paired controls with battery power and settings unchanged. No gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established.", "source_hash": "0e428f343d60f7adb08f3627e633c382df52ec6417375aeeff58cc18c9916dca"}], "trials": [{"name": "libxsmm-reference-with-unused-panel32", "hypothesis": "Original full512 GEMM body plus an optional unused generated panel primitive", "source_hash": "e7b5eb8a9b290651137639917a3bdca342e9a1ed767d0ff8718449b03214c200", "status": "passed", "scores": [1034.6008060042761, 1071.2393688287348, 1048.7480498774569], "control_scores": [], "promoted": null, "adjudication": null}, {"name": "libxsmm-n512-sixteen-panel32-calls", "hypothesis": "Replace the n=512 full-kernel call with sixteen supplied panel32 calls. Bands r=0,32,...,480 cover all output rows exactly once; slots [4]=B, [10]=A+r*512 and [16]=C+r*512 implement the supplied row-major mapping. Other slots remain zero. This tests 64 KiB per-call packing against additional call and SME transition costs. No fallible setup is added; the original full512 kernel remains available unchanged. General sizes, tails, n<=0, FP32 arithmetic and both complete license notices remain unchanged. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness and performance are untested. Promotion requires fresh paired controls under the fixed gates and unchanged battery power/settings; throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is not established.", "source_hash": "0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051", "status": "passed", "scores": [1078.0540847204095, 1078.5954894302113, 1090.8285440982768], "control_scores": [1112.3031817250492, 1073.5657393811312, 1066.9814194878586], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Recorded correctness validation passed, but eligible=false prohibits adoption; promoted=false confirms no promotion. Candidate scores were 1078.1\u20131090.8 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 1067.0\u20131112.3. Paired changes were \u22123.1%, +0.5%, and +2.2%. Candidate and control spreads were 12.8 and 45.3 GFLOP/s. These mixed, overlapping results establish no repeatable gain. No repeat exceeded 1800 GFLOP/s. Retain the unchanged full512 incumbent. Carry the panel layout forward only as an unproven hypothesis for the next swarm review; these measurements do not isolate packing, cache, or SME transition costs. Preserve the fixed gates, pending experiment order, remaining budget, and separate power-mode records."}}, {"name": "libxsmm-n512-constant-shape-c-sme", "hypothesis": "Route n=512 through the existing full A transpose and a guarded C SME implementation with fixed 32x32 tiles and 512 K steps. Each tile remains in ZA for its full FP32 accumulation. The guard requires 16 streaming FP32 lanes; other vector lengths retain the general loop. Allocation failure, other sizes, tails and n<=0 retain existing fallback behavior. This tests compiler-visible constant bounds. The full512 assembly, panel helper and complete license notices remain unchanged. This source is absent from the supplied measured history. Compilation, correctness and performance are untested; no gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established. Validate the full timed GEMM under the fixed gates with fresh repeated paired battery controls and unchanged settings. Results compare the complete changed path and cannot isolate constant bounds from the change in implementation.", "source_hash": "cadb05ebb2492c7eda03d5a5e2a71eca07ce0a5bcfd4e4a83053f708a3c598b7", "status": "passed", "scores": [1047.2138413825999, 760.4403942913434, 1082.4010468993888], "control_scores": [1077.3314837168991, 1060.133943762729, 1077.5131603180137], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Recorded correctness validation passed, but eligible=false prohibits adoption; promoted=false confirms no promotion. Candidate scores were 760.4\u20131082.4 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 1060.1\u20131077.5. Paired changes were \u22122.8%, \u221228.3%, and +0.5%. Candidate and control repeat spreads were 322.0 and 17.4 GFLOP/s. Two pairs lost, and the sole gain was smaller than the control spread. These results establish no repeatable improvement; no repeat exceeded 1800 GFLOP/s. Retain the unchanged full512 incumbent. Treat the constant-shape C SME path as unproven for the next swarm review. The measurements do not isolate effects from constant bounds, transpose, allocation, or implementation choice. Preserve the fixed gates, pending experiment order, remaining budget, and separate AC/battery records."}}, {"name": "libxsmm-full512-padded-272-byte-a-slices", "hypothesis": "Implement the selected padding experiment in the full512 kernel: allocate 139264 bytes, pack 512 slices at 272-byte intervals, and update both packed-A consumer strides and rewinds. Each slice contains 256 data bytes followed by 16 unused bytes. The second row half still starts at byte 128; the final data byte is at offset 139247, within the allocation. B and C addressing, FP32 FMOPA order, one kernel call, SME transitions, general-size fallback, and both complete license notices remain unchanged. This corrected layout is absent from the supplied measured history. The hypothesis is that padding changes scratch cache mapping; cache geometry and benefit are unverified. No tools or tests were run. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness, and performance require fresh validation under the fixed gates and repeated paired controls with battery power and settings unchanged. No gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established.", "source_hash": "0e428f343d60f7adb08f3627e633c382df52ec6417375aeeff58cc18c9916dca", "status": "passed", "scores": [968.0605424189555, 1027.1778007638068, 836.0313468612225], "control_scores": [1097.334515870469, 935.4488286473301, 1057.6986775348985], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Recorded correctness validation passed, but eligible=false prohibits adoption; promoted=false confirms no promotion. Candidate throughput was 836.0\u20131027.2 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 935.4\u20131097.3. Paired changes were \u221211.8%, +9.8%, and \u221221.0%. Candidate and control repeat spreads were 191.1 and 161.9 GFLOP/s. Two pairs lost; these mixed results establish no repeatable gain. No repeat exceeded 1800 GFLOP/s. Retain the unchanged full512 incumbent. Treat 272-byte scratch padding as unproven for the next swarm review; these measurements do not establish a cache mechanism or isolate the cause of variation. Preserve the fixed gates, pending experiment order, remaining budget, and separate AC/battery records."}}]}, {"phase": "cpu-libxsmm-automatic-2026-09-25", "calls": 3, "implementations": 0, "profile": {"cpu": "Apple M4 Pro", "power": "Battery Power", "energy_mode": "Automatic", "capabilities": ["cpu", "arm64", "sme", "sme2", "single-thread"]}, "host": {"timestamp_utc": "2026-09-25T09:46:43.909119+00:00", "battery": "Now drawing from 'Battery Power'\n -InternalBattery-0 (id=21889123)\t39%; discharging; 1:33 remaining present: true\n", "power_settings": "Battery Power:\n powermode            0\n Sleep On Power Button 1\n standby              1\n ttyskeepawake        1\n hibernatemode        3\n powernap             0\n hibernatefile        /var/vm/sleepimage\n displaysleep         10\n womp                 0\n networkoversleep     0\n sleep                3\n tcpkeepalive         1\n lessbright           1\n disksleep            10\nAC Power:\n Sleep On Power Button 1\n powermode            0\n standby              1\n ttyskeepawake        1\n hibernatemode        3\n powernap             1\n hibernatefile        /var/vm/sleepimage\n displaysleep         10\n womp                 1\n networkoversleep     0\n sleep                5\n tcpkeepalive         1\n disksleep            10\n", "thermal": "Note: No thermal warning level has been recorded\nNote: No performance warning level has been recorded\nNote: No CPU power status has been recorded\n"}, "status": "final-performance-unconfirmed", "attempts": [], "trials": [{"name": "libxsmm-reference-with-unused-panel32", "hypothesis": "Original full512 GEMM body plus an optional unused generated panel primitive", "source_hash": "e7b5eb8a9b290651137639917a3bdca342e9a1ed767d0ff8718449b03214c200", "status": "passed", "scores": [1395.376025183649, 1668.5964359101147, 1638.0501111326396], "control_scores": [], "promoted": null, "adjudication": null}, {"name": "libxsmm-n512-sixteen-panel32-calls", "hypothesis": "Replace the n=512 full-kernel call with sixteen supplied panel32 calls. Bands r=0,32,...,480 cover all output rows exactly once; slots [4]=B, [10]=A+r*512 and [16]=C+r*512 implement the supplied row-major mapping. Other slots remain zero. This tests 64 KiB per-call packing against additional call and SME transition costs. No fallible setup is added; the original full512 kernel remains available unchanged. General sizes, tails, n<=0, FP32 arithmetic and both complete license notices remain unchanged. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness and performance are untested. Promotion requires fresh paired controls under the fixed gates and unchanged battery power/settings; throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is not established.", "source_hash": "0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051", "status": "passed", "scores": [1339.665422049814, 1630.1715449692645, 1410.961601509877], "control_scores": [1503.4863055257904, 1384.8800912868048, 1236.317618652453], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Retain the incumbent: the fixed evaluator marked this candidate ineligible. Recorded correctness passed, but fresh Automatic battery paired differences were \u221210.9%, +17.7%, and +14.1%. Candidate scores ranged from 1339.7\u20131630.2 GFLOP/s; controls ranged from 1236.3\u20131503.5. These variable results do not establish a repeatable optimization gain over the imported LIBXSMM reference. No held-out result is supplied. Any revision must pass the unchanged correctness, paired-repeat, promotion, and held-out gates."}}, {"name": "libxsmm-n512-constant-shape-c-sme", "hypothesis": "Route n=512 through the existing full A transpose and a guarded C SME implementation with fixed 32x32 tiles and 512 K steps. Each tile remains in ZA for its full FP32 accumulation. The guard requires 16 streaming FP32 lanes; other vector lengths retain the general loop. Allocation failure, other sizes, tails and n<=0 retain existing fallback behavior. This tests compiler-visible constant bounds. The full512 assembly, panel helper and complete license notices remain unchanged. This source is absent from the supplied measured history. Compilation, correctness and performance are untested; no gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established. Validate the full timed GEMM under the fixed gates with fresh repeated paired battery controls and unchanged settings. Results compare the complete changed path and cannot isolate constant bounds from the change in implementation.", "source_hash": "cadb05ebb2492c7eda03d5a5e2a71eca07ce0a5bcfd4e4a83053f708a3c598b7", "status": "passed", "scores": [1488.5541912181227, 1207.1297008551162, 1270.9530036238923], "control_scores": [1628.115076394983, 1282.845623880019, 1632.2333595813482], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "reject", "reason": "The fixed evaluator marked this candidate ineligible. Recorded correctness passed, but fresh Automatic battery throughput was below its paired control in all three repeats: \u22128.6%, \u22125.9%, and \u221222.1%. Candidate scores ranged from 1207.1\u20131488.6 GFLOP/s; controls ranged from 1282.8\u20131632.2. Despite substantial repeat variation, no pair showed a gain over the imported LIBXSMM reference. No held-out result is supplied. Discard this candidate and retain the incumbent. These measurements do not isolate the effects of constant bounds, transpose, or implementation choice."}}, {"name": "libxsmm-full512-padded-272-byte-a-slices", "hypothesis": "Implement the selected padding experiment in the full512 kernel: allocate 139264 bytes, pack 512 slices at 272-byte intervals, and update both packed-A consumer strides and rewinds. Each slice contains 256 data bytes followed by 16 unused bytes. The second row half still starts at byte 128; the final data byte is at offset 139247, within the allocation. B and C addressing, FP32 FMOPA order, one kernel call, SME transitions, general-size fallback, and both complete license notices remain unchanged. This corrected layout is absent from the supplied measured history. The hypothesis is that padding changes scratch cache mapping; cache geometry and benefit are unverified. No tools or tests were run. Compilation, full-GEMM correctness, and performance require fresh validation under the fixed gates and repeated paired controls with battery power and settings unchanged. No gain or throughput above 1800 GFLOP/s is established.", "source_hash": "0e428f343d60f7adb08f3627e633c382df52ec6417375aeeff58cc18c9916dca", "status": "passed", "scores": [1252.907603299889, 1252.907603299889, 1377.185282533294], "control_scores": [1132.4383837853318, 1549.7777397605503, 1441.2641417233476], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "reject", "reason": "The fixed evaluator marked this candidate ineligible. Recorded correctness passed, but fresh Automatic battery paired differences were +10.6%, \u221219.2%, and \u22124.4%. Candidate throughput ranged from 1252.9\u20131377.2 GFLOP/s; controls ranged from 1132.4\u20131549.8. This variation and two losing pairs do not establish a repeatable gain over the imported LIBXSMM reference. No held-out result is supplied. Discard this candidate and retain the incumbent. These scores do not establish a cache-mapping cause or a power-mode kernel speedup."}}]}]
Source reviews:
{"packing-review-luna.md": "# Unmeasured LIBXSMM kernel directions for the next board\n\n## Baseline facts\n\nThe pinned baseline calls the full 512x512 LIBXSMM kernel once from [`baseline/kernel.c`](../cpu-libxsmm-generator-2026-09-25/baseline/kernel.c#L769-L780). It enters SME once. In its assembly, `Llibxsmm_64` allocates a 128 KiB stack buffer and `Llibxsmm_78` fills that buffer before the FMOPA loop (`baseline/kernel.c` lines 69-75 onward). The optional N=32 panel helper is embedded at line 823 but the baseline does not call it.\n\nPrior results are not evidence of a winner. The 16-call N=32 panel candidate passed correctness but was not promoted: paired differences were mixed in Low Power (\u22123.1%, +0.5%, +2.2%) and Automatic (\u221210.9%, +17.7%, +14.1%). The fixed-size C SME candidate was also not promoted. Padding A slices to 272 bytes lost two of three paired comparisons in each mode and was rejected. The Automatic baseline itself failed held-out confirmation. See the two `search/result.json` files in `cpu-libxsmm-panel-swarm-2026-09-25` and `cpu-libxsmm-automatic-2026-09-25`.\n\n## Distinct hypotheses for the board\n\n1. **Use N=64 output-row panels instead of sixteen N=32 panels.** This requires a separately host-prepared `(M,N,K)=(512,64,512)` FP32 beta-zero helper. After that helper is supplied, the source edit is to change the N=512 wrapper loop at [`trial-001/source/kernel.c`](../cpu-libxsmm-panel-swarm-2026-09-25/search/trial-001/source/kernel.c#L774-L783) to advance `r` by 64 and call it eight times. Keep `A_arg=A+r*512`, `B_arg=B`, and `C_arg=C+r*512`. This is legal because it partitions disjoint output rows; the host must confirm the pinned generator emits the exact shape and validate the helper before the Sera run. **Hypothesis:** fewer calls and SME start/stop transitions may offset the larger per-call panel work. It is unmeasured; N=32 results do not predict it. Astra's 180-second implementation call has no filesystem, compiler, or generator tools, so it cannot prepare this helper itself.\n\n2. **Split the other output axis with an M=32 helper.** This requires a separately host-prepared `(32,512,512)` helper. Once supplied, cover the original output in sixteen column bands. For band `c`, pass column-major `A_arg=B+c`, `B_arg=A`, and `C_arg=C+c`, with leading dimensions still 512; this computes the matching 32 rows of `C\u1d40=B\u1d40A\u1d40`. This changes which operand is panelized and how C is written, unlike the tested N=32 row-band layout. **Hypothesis:** its pack and cache behavior may differ. The host must verify the M-tail generator path and guarded output boundaries before timing. Astra can make the wrapper edit within its implementation cap only after the validated helper is present; correctness and gains remain unknown.\n\n3. **Use a transpose-aware full-shape kernel with a matching prepacked input layout.** The pinned generator supports transpose descriptor flags, but a flag-only change is mathematically wrong for the existing row-major pointers. Host preparation must supply a generated full-shape variant and its verified descriptor/layout contract before Sera edits the wrapper to pack the matching input; all packing work must remain inside the timed call. **Hypothesis:** packing once into a reusable layout may cost less than the current internal stack packing at `Llibxsmm_78`. This needs preparation beyond the existing 512x32 helper, which Astra cannot generate. The host should verify the mapping, beta-zero/output semantics, and correctness before a timed Sera run. This is separate from the failed 272-byte scratch-padding experiment, which changed scratch spacing without removing packing.\n\nOne level of Strassen is mathematically compatible with FP32 GEMM only if its changed rounding stays within the fixed tolerance. It needs host preparation for suitable subproduct helpers; the available primitive is 512x32x512, not the 256x256 subproducts Strassen needs. The implementation also adds seven products, temporary sums, and new packing. Include it as a research alternative only if the board wants to investigate it, with helper preparation and validation explicitly assigned outside Astra's tool-limited call. Do not assume it is a win.\n\nThese are alternatives for board ranking, not a selected experiment or performance claim. Keep the full product, FP32, single-thread, frozen-hill, correctness, paired-control, promotion, and power-state rules unchanged.\n", "compute-review-luna.md": "# Remaining source-grounded hypotheses for the next board\n\nThis is a read-only review of `cpu-libxsmm-generator-2026-09-25/baseline/kernel.c`, the prior `layout-review-luna.md`, and the completed panel-swarm and Automatic replay records. It proposes candidate questions only. It does not rank or select them. No observed result identifies a compute, memory, or streaming bottleneck.\n\n## Evidence already tested\n\nThe original full-size kernel remains the incumbent. In both `Llibxsmm_478` and `Llibxsmm_70c`, each K iteration loads two vector pairs, advances the pointers, executes four FP32 FMOPA instructions into ZA0\u2013ZA3, decrements the counter, and branches. The kernel packs its operand into 128 KiB of stack scratch, then runs two 512-K loops for its output halves.\n\nThe completed `next-continue` block already tested three nearby K-loop schedules: counter decrement before FMOPA, pointer updates after FMOPA, and pointer updates interleaved with FMOPA. None promoted. Do not reissue these as separate proposals. The panel-swarm tested sixteen 32-row helper calls, a C SME fallback with constant 512 bounds, and 272-byte padded A slices. All passed correctness but none promoted. Automatic replay used the exact same sources and also promoted none; its higher, highly variable scores do not prove a source improvement or a power-mode effect. The detailed reports do not provide stage timings or hardware counters.\n\n## Distinct implementation questions for specialists\n\n### Two-K pipeline: already tested; only loop-control simplification is distinct\n\nRoot checked the actual `ec83ff3b\u2026` source diff in `cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24`: both loops already load K0 into z0\u2013z3 and K1 into z4\u2013z7, issue the eight FMOPAs in contribution order, and advance two strides. The earlier claim that this second bank was untested was incorrect. Do not propose that pipeline again as a new mechanism.\n\nThe tested source retains a compare/branch and an unreachable odd-K tail for the fixed K=512 shape. A separate, narrow hypothesis is to use a 256-iteration loop over the existing two-K body and remove that unnecessary tail/control logic. This reduces loop-control instructions only; the two-bank load schedule is already measured. Whether that smaller change matters is unknown. Specialists may rank or reject it without attributing old pipeline behavior to a new experiment.\n\n### Operand orientation using a generator-supported transpose descriptor (preparation required)\n\n**Source fact:** The pinned LIBXSMM SME generator supports transpose descriptor flags, but the legal mapping requires the physical input layout and leading dimensions to agree with the flags. The current row-major ABI is implemented through the column-major identity `C\u1d40 = B\u1d40 A\u1d40`. The generator review documents legal descriptor and panel choices; a flag-only flip on the current pointers would compute the wrong operation. Prior C SME and panel-helper candidates do not test this descriptor/layout pairing in the original one-call 512\u00d7512 generated kernel.\n\n**Candidate:** Export one full 512\u00d7512 beta-zero SME primitive with a changed transpose flag and explicitly matched operand buffer/pointer mapping. Write the exact logical element equations and leading dimensions before changing the wrapper. Any transpose or pack needed to create the matching storage must happen inside `gemm` and remain timed; preserve output overwrite semantics and the unchanged non-512 fallback. Do not assume a flag avoids the current pack: inspect the emitted code and confirm which transpose/pack path is actually removed or added before proposing the measurement.\n\n**Hypothesis, not a result:** A different operand orientation may change or remove packing and improve access. Explicit conversion may simply move the cost, and the generator source only establishes legality\u2014not a benefit for the exact full-size call. This is not directly executable by the current Astra implementation agent: exporting and validating a new primitive requires host-side LIBXSMM generator access. Treat it as a preparation task for a later board, not as a current 180-second implementation candidate.\n\n### Keep packed scratch reserved through its consumers (lifetime invariant)\n\n**Source fact:** At `Llibxsmm_64`, `x20` saves the incoming stack pointer, the code reserves 128 KiB, and `x3`/`x26` hold the scratch base. `Llibxsmm_78` writes the packed data through `[sp]` while advancing `sp` upward by 0x100-byte stores until it returns to the saved top. The current `mov sp,x20` then restores the stack pointer before `Llibxsmm_478` and `Llibxsmm_70c` consume the still-addressable region through `x3`. Apple documents a 128-byte ARM64 stack red zone below SP; the packed 128 KiB region is larger and is outside that guarantee ([Apple ARM64 ABI guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/writing-arm64-code-for-apple-platforms)). The assembly makes no intervening C calls or explicit stack stores, so the available evidence does not show a correctness failure; it does show that most of the scratch storage is no longer represented as live stack allocation during its reads.\n\n**Candidate invariant repair:** Keep SP at the allocated scratch base throughout both packing and compute. Use a dedicated available integer register as the packing-store cursor instead of incrementing SP; preserve the cursor's ABI obligations. Keep x26 as the original scratch base and x3 as the compute cursor. Restore SP from x20 only after both consumers, before the outer loop's next allocation. Merely resetting SP after packing protects compute but leaves the packing-stage lifetime gap. Validate exact control-flow placement and all eight packing stores/increments before evaluation.\n\n**Classification:** This is a stack-lifetime/ABI audit question, not a performance hypothesis. No failure or speed impact has been measured. It must not be presented as a demonstrated optimization.\n\n## Constraints for any board proposal\n\n- Treat the source facts above separately from performance explanations. The noisy same-source and paired scores do not establish cache conflicts, load stalls, stack reuse, or power-mode causality.\n- Keep the frozen 512 FP32 beta-zero workload, arithmetic correctness tolerance, full-call timing, general-size fallback, and complete license text. Keep all packing, conversions, allocation, and SME transitions inside the measured call.\n- Avoid repeat candidates equivalent to the tested panel32 wrapper, constant-bound C SME, 272-byte padding, or the three measured K-loop instruction schedules.\n- Require exact pointer/leading-dimension equations for any transpose or layout proposal, and preserve fresh paired controls and the existing promotion/holdout gates.\n\n## Evidence paths\n\n- Baseline source and generator context: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-generator-2026-09-25/baseline/kernel.c`, `generator-review-luna.md`, `layout-review-luna.md`.\n- Panel outcomes: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-panel-swarm-2026-09-25/README.md`, `agent/state.json`, and `search/trial-001..003`.\n- Automatic exact-source replay: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-automatic-2026-09-25/README.md`, `agent/state.json`, and `search/trial-001..003`.\n- Previously tested K-loop schedules: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-next-continue-2026-09-25/README.md` and `search/result.json`.\n", "history-review-luna.md": "# LIBXSMM experiment history for the next board\n\nThis inventory distinguishes **actual source hashes** from proposal text, abstentions, and timeouts. Every candidate below is unpromoted. The exact low-power-to-Automatic replay is a second measurement of the same four panel-swarm source hashes, not four new implementations.\n\n## Implemented source changes\n\n| Exact source SHA-256 | Actual change | Evidence and disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `7e9ab1076777a40cb0ad30527e381b221d4024cd99f370708971fb652b31810e` | Imported LIBXSMM full-512 SME kernel with row-major wrapper and general-size fallback. This is the original standalone reference, not a Sera optimization. | AC reference baseline, AC swarm baseline-only, battery reference, and next-continuation baseline; target not met. |\n| `bf47e981d23289bfc6faba38d6ac5a3c8362db81f2b61e0215dd441135878685` | In both full-512 K loops, add one-step lookahead loads into z4\u2013z7, copy them into z0\u2013z3, and drain K=511. | Prepared-only phase passed correctness but was stopped before performance scoring. Later battery phase measured it and rejected it; no promotion. This is one change, not a separate experiment in each phase. |\n| `8b01ee298ba0f0d49915cec8e0730ff168af64562bb9d6f717f7adbaaba5ff3a` | In both K loops, replace the 512-step decrement/branch with end pointers and pointer comparisons for the A/B streams. | Battery phase measured; mixed candidate/control repeats, revised and not promoted. |\n| `ec83ff3b94f2983c17a1f7fd69aea377ed143b5993635a8770818124d4fcdd91` | In both K loops, load two K slices and issue eight ordered FMOPAs per loop body, with a one-step tail. | Battery phase measured; three paired gains varied widely, revised and not promoted. |\n| `71c55f82b9955eefda8ca17f47e59fbe04c98919c7d30d110a6c540f38005bb8` | Move each K-loop counter decrement from after the FMOPA group to before it. | Next-continuation phase measured; revised and not promoted. |\n| `13bc6a2ba0dc662d722b0ba8d09db1f2fc55ef77396b1056243d5fb154bf3cdc` | Move both K-loop pointer increments from before the FMOPA group to after it. | Next-continuation phase measured; revised and not promoted. |\n| `a748475df6036ec229169ae103e91d567ca0854e45d885c5c8887c2e9580c36a` | Interleave the x0/x3 pointer increments within each four-FMOPA group: x0 after the first FMOPA, x3 after the third. | Next-continuation phase measured; revised and not promoted. |\n| `e7b5eb8a9b290651137639917a3bdca342e9a1ed767d0ff8718449b03214c200` | Imported full-512 LIBXSMM body plus an additional generated 32-row panel primitive that the baseline wrapper does not call. | Panel-swarm baseline. This is a prepared/imported comparison source, not a Sera-discovered speedup and not hash `7e9ab1076777a40cb0ad30527e381b221d4024cd99f370708971fb652b31810e`. |\n| `0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051` | Replace one `sera_libxsmm_512` call in `gemm(n=512)` with sixteen calls to the supplied `sera_libxsmm_panel32`, one per 32-row output band. | Panel-swarm candidate, measured and unpromoted. Automatic replay reran the exact hash; it is not a new change. |\n| `cadb05ebb2492c7eda03d5a5e2a71eca07ce0a5bcfd4e4a83053f708a3c598b7` | Route n=512 through a C SME implementation: retain the A transpose, accumulate fixed 32\u00d732 tiles over K=512 in four ZA tiles, then store C. | Panel-swarm candidate, measured and unpromoted. Automatic replay reran the exact hash. |\n| `0e428f343d60f7adb08f3627e633c382df52ec6417375aeeff58cc18c9916dca` | Keep the full-512 call but pad each packed-A K slice from 256 to 272 bytes; enlarge stack scratch and update consumer strides/rewinds. | Panel-swarm candidate, measured and unpromoted. Automatic replay reran the exact hash. |\n\nThe three panel-swarm candidates above retain the same order and hashes in `cpu-libxsmm-automatic-2026-09-25`; Automatic adds no source change. Their scores and paired controls are in each phase's `search/result.json`.\n\n## Selected work that produced no candidate\n\n- `cpu-libxsmm-swarm-2026-09-24`: the first selected K-loop schedule implementation timed out at 180 seconds. It produced no source. The same ranked experiment was later implemented as `bf47e981d23289bfc6faba38d6ac5a3c8362db81f2b61e0215dd441135878685` and performance-tested.\n- `cpu-libxsmm-swarm-edits-2026-09-24`: a guarded-edit retry produced that same `bf47e981d23289bfc6faba38d6ac5a3c8362db81f2b61e0215dd441135878685` source and passed 48 correctness cases, but the AC preflight stopped the scorer because the host was on battery. It did not produce a second candidate or performance score.\n- `cpu-libxsmm-next-2026-09-25`: the selected A-transpose implementation timed out at 180 seconds before producing a source. Its ranked queue was preserved for continuation.\n- `cpu-libxsmm-next-continue-2026-09-25`: a second selected SME/tile implementation timed out and produced no source. The other tested K-loop sources are listed above.\n- The battery phase's selected 64\u00d732 cache-blocking proposal abstained because the full-512 reference already traverses 64 output rows as two 32-row tiles for each B panel. The selected predicated paired-LD1W post-index proposal abstained because that load form cannot update these two pointers.\n- A selected scratch-lifetime proposal sought to reduce the full-512 128 KiB stack panel to 64 KiB while preserving the current consumer and traversal. The current code advances scratch by 256 bytes per K step and accesses through byte 130,943; a smaller allocation would overrun it. A valid reduced-buffer scheme needs coordinated packing, stride, rewind, and traversal changes outside that selected scope.\n- The panel board's first selected panel32 idea was implemented once. Later panel-packing/cache-blocking attempts abstained as exact duplicates of the sixteen-call wrapper. A cache-layout proposal also abstained because its proposed 2,112-byte stride, 128 KiB-plus-8 KiB footprint, and 65,664-byte footprint were mutually inconsistent. The later measured `0e428f343d60f7adb08f3627e633c382df52ec6417375aeeff58cc18c9916dca` implementation used the corrected 272-byte stride; do not list that correction as untested.\n\nOther specialist abstentions cite no distinct source-grounded proposal, existing full-kernel vector/tile behavior, or lack of compiler/disassembly evidence. These are recorded in the phase `agent/state.json` files; they are not failed measurements.\n\n## Baseline and prompt corrections\n\n1. Keep `7e9ab1076777a40cb0ad30527e381b221d4024cd99f370708971fb652b31810e` (the original exported LIBXSMM reference) separate from `e7b5eb8a9b290651137639917a3bdca342e9a1ed767d0ff8718449b03214c200` (that reference plus an unused generated panel helper). The latter was the panel-swarm replay baseline. `0b3b4431f6de2c249f799b21fa2e6731a4353a8cf303b8ec36d2e3b8b86b2051` is the first source here that changes the n=512 wrapper to call the helper sixteen times.\n2. Do not describe the old 1,160\u20131,449 GFLOP/s result from the separate AutoLab `vvennela/kernel` hill as Sera package evidence. It came from another agent/task and another submission. The user-reported 1,780 GFLOP/s result belongs to that same AutoLab hill and is not Sera package evidence.\n3. Claims that the 64\u00d732 traversal, paired predicated LD1W, four ZA accumulator tiles, and panel32 primitive are wholly absent from the relevant baseline are misleading. Check the exact baseline hash and code block: some are already in the original kernel; panel32 was present but unused in `e7b5eb8a9b290651137639917a3bdca342e9a1ed767d0ff8718449b03214c200`.\n4. Treat cross-phase scores as historical context only. Battery Low Power and Automatic blocks used the same four panel hashes, but no candidate was promoted in either. The sequential mode comparison does not identify a power-mode cause or a kernel speedup.\n\nUse these exclusions as coverage context only. This review does not select a role, proposal, or experiment order for the next 15-Luna board.\n\n\n## Primary records\n\n- Original source: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-reference-2026-09-24/editable/kernel.c` and `baseline-run/search/result.json`.\n- K-loop history: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/result.json`, `agent/state.json`, and `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-next-continue-2026-09-25/search/result.json`, `agent/state.json`.\n- Prepared-only retry: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-swarm-edits-2026-09-24/prepared/kernel.c` and `agent/state.json`.\n- Panel baseline/candidates: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-panel-swarm-2026-09-25/search/result.json` and `agent/state.json`; Automatic replay: `evidence/cpu-libxsmm-automatic-2026-09-25/search/result.json`.\n"}
